 in my laboratory  in other words having a panel of uh  impox virus along with HSV  uh potentially vzv as a cause of skin  lesions so that we can perform that uh  screen and and rule out on on different  uh skin dermal lesions for uh what we've  considered common causes of dermal  genital lesions and now impox virus  which we can include in that in that  list so I really encourage  um  commercial manufacturers to consider  panels because I think that they would  be a valuable tool for Physicians and  clinical laboratories  great thank you that brings me to my  next question the initial symptom in  patients with monkey pox can manifest as  oropharyngeal symptoms which include  pharyngitis  epiglottitis and Odin Aphasia how do I  send an oral sample for testing to the  laboratory  yeah so if a patient has  lesions in their mouth or throat  that a physician suspects could be due  to Monkey pox it's appropriate to swab  that lesion and send it in just as they  would a dermal or genital lesion  that's appropriate to collect it's  appropriate to test because as was  brought up in the question  patients can develop lesions in the  mouth and throat  especially after receptive oral sex  being a means of transmitting epox virus  so that that would be the recommended  testing approach  great thank you our next question is  would that be sexual transmission in  general or rather between men  um so this is an important issue to  bring up and discuss  although early on in the outbreak many  of the cases of impacts were in the men  who have sex with men population  it is inaccurate to refer to this as a  disease that is specific to that  population because we have seen impacts  virus infections occurring in other  populations as well anyone who comes in  close contact with a an infected lesion  can contract the disease it's even still  a question of whether impox virus should  be categorized as a sexually transmitted  disease or not there are other routes  that this virus can be transmitted by we  talked about contaminated fomites being  a source of transmission  so while we know that this virus can be  transmitted through close intimate  contact including sexual contact there  are other routes that can result in the  virus being transmitted so that's why  it's really important to be careful  about the way that we talk about  transmission routes with this particular  infection  thank you our next question is do the  latest smallpox and npox vaccines cause  blistering and Scar formations like the  old smallpox vaccines  yeah I I think the answer to that is  know that we're not seeing the same type  of of blistering as we saw with patients  you know decades ago who were receiving  smallpox vaccines  um  so I don't think that that is a uh as a  concern as as it was in the past  great my next  would those manifestations that appear  to be somewhat irregular be due to the  Epic the endemic aspects of where it  originated in one of those two areas in  question in Africa or would there be  another reason or cause  yeah  so in general infections due to clade 2  which was previously the the West  African have been more minor in terms of  the the disease severity and the  mortality rate and that is the clade  that is causing the current outbreak and  so I think that's uh a possible  explanation as to why we're seeing a  different a range of of severity of  lesions whereas clade 1 or that Central  African Congo base in clade usually is  associated with a more severe disease  presentation  more lesions and higher mortality rates  so I think that there likely is an  aspect of the the type the strain of the  virus that's causing the current  outbreak with the um in most situations  less severe type of clinical  presentation  great thank you our next question is why  are dry swabs or those collected in vtn  compatible with testing but not swabs in  UTM  that is a great question and we we've  asked that to  the CDC and  it comes down to the validation that the  Centers for Disease Control  performed and they've specifically  indicated to performing Laboratories  that dry swabs and swabs and viral  transport media specifically are the  ones that are approved whereas swabs in  Universal transport media are not  considered on label for the CDC test  um I think that myself along with you  know many Clinical Laboratory directors  feel that there is a lot of similarity  between viral transport media and  Universal transport media but currently  we're only accepting swabs and vtm for  that reason I think if a lab wants to  perform some comparison studies and show  that swabs and UTM is acceptable  that that is a possibility with a  bridging study and if companies also  want to develop and validate swabs in  UTM now that there's the eua process  that that uh  opens it up for more flexibility with  regards to the type of transport media  that can be used  great thank you our next question is in  September the CDC issued guidance for  concern that the California variant of  monkey pox contained a mutation that  affected the target PCR Gene for the CDC  West African monkey pox assay how  frequent is this variant have you seen  it in samples submitted to the Mayo  Clinic for testing  yeah I'm glad this question came up and  there have been a number of uh studies  and a recent publication showing that  there can be instances where the virus  may have a deletion present that can  render certain molecular tests  as  unable to detect that particular variant  I don't think the frequency is high  and fortunately the majority of testing  that's been used to date has not been  for specific clades where we're seeing  this deletion issue cause more problems  but instead most of the testing is being  done at the genus level which seems to  um in some ways sidestep the deletion  issue but it's something that we  continue to need to monitor as we saw  with covid-19 there are different  variants and strains in circulation and  Laboratories and Commercial  manufacturers will need to continue to  sequence these strains and ensure that  the assays that they're using are able  to detect these strains effectively  great thank you and it looks like we  have time for one more question today  and that question is which type of swab  is the most useful for mpox diagnosis  so if the if the question is to the  exact type of swab  um I would refer  um the participants to go to the CDC  website if they're sending samples to a  laboratory that's using the CDC test  um  the CDC outlines that there are certain  types of swabs like cotton swabs that  should not be used  but dermal and genital swabs are the  preferred specimen type I know that some  commercial manufacturers are validating  specific commercial swabs or maybe even  their own proprietary swab and transport  media so it really depends on the test  as to what type of swab and then  potentially transport media is going to  be a useful for diagnosis  great thank you and it looks like that  brings us to the end of our q a session  for today thank you Dr biniker do you  have any final comments for our audience  just appreciate everybody taking the  time to join me today for this uh  overview of of impox and I hope that um  the rest of the year goes smoothly for  everybody in the 2023 will be uh quieter  and we can focus on  um what we uh what we want to focus on  instead of emerging infectious diseases  thank you  yes thank you again Dr biniker for your  time today and your important research  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor CG Technologies for  underwriting today's educational webcast  before we go I'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  we did not have time for today and those  submitted during the on-demand period  will be addressed by the speaker via the  contact information you provided at the  time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  words will alert you via emai